t tangent (Lemma 2).\n\n*Step 3 – Homothety with centre \\(A\\) sending \\((PMN)\\) to \\((BEF)\\).*  \nSince \\(PA=PE=PF\\), the ratios \\(\\displaystyle k=\\frac{AE}{AP}=\\frac{AF}{AP}\\) are equal.  The homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) centred at \\(A\\) with ratio \\(k\\) sends \\(P\\) to \\(E\\) and \\(O\\) (the centre of \\((PMN)\\)) to the centre of \\((BEF)\\).  Moreover \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), to themselves; consequently \\(\\mathcal H\\bigl((PMN)\\bigr)=(BEF)\\) (Lemma 3).\n\n*Step 4 – Conclusion.*  \nA homothety maps a tangent line to a tangent line.  Applying \\(\\mathcal H\\) to the external common tangent found in Lemma 2 yields a line tangent to \\((BEF)\\).  Because \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixes the direction of the line (it is centred at \\(A\\) and only rescales distances), the image line is still parallel to \\(AP\\).  Hence the line through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as required.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) respectively, \\(|\\Omega|<|\\Gamma|\\);  \n* \\(A,B\\) be their two intersection points;  \n* \\(C\\neq M\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(D\\neq N\\) be the second intersection of the same line with \\(\\Gamma\\) (the order on the line is \\(C-M-N-D\\));  \n* \\(P\\) be the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);  \n* \\(F\\neq A\\) be the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);  \n* \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\nWe shall prove that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1.  The quadrilateral \\(AEFC\\) is cyclic with centre \\(P\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(P\\) is the circumcenter of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), we have  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PD. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line \\(AP\\); by definition \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\).  \nBecause \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\), (1) gives  \n\n\\[\nPA=PC=PE,\\qquad PA=PD=PF .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(PA=PC=PE=PF\\); i.e. the four points \\(A,C,E,F\\) are all at the same distance from \\(P\\).  Hence they lie on the circle centred at \\(P\\) with radius \\(PA\\).  ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2.  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external common tangent of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\nLet \\(O\\) be the midpoint of the segment \\(MN\\); \\(O\\) is the centre of the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\).  \nDenote by \\(H'\\) the reflection of \\(H\\) in the side \\(MN\\).  In any triangle the reflection of the orthocenter in a side lies on the circumcircle; therefore  \n\n\\[\nH'\\in (PMN). \\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the power of a point \\(X\\) on the line \\(AP\\) with respect to the two circles \\((AEFC)\\) (centre \\(P\\)) and \\((PMN)\\) (centre \\(O\\)) is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(AEFC)}(X)=XP^{2}-PA^{2},\\qquad\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{(PMN)}(X)=XO^{2}-OA^{2}.\n\\]\n\nFor the particular point \\(A\\) both powers are zero.  Since the two circles intersect at \\(A\\) and at a second point (the reflection of \\(A\\) across \\(MN\\)), the set of points having equal powers with respect to the two circles is exactly their radical axis.  Consequently the radical axis of \\((AEFC)\\) and \\((PMN)\\) is the line through \\(A\\) that is orthogonal to the line joining their centres, i.e. the line through \\(A\\) orthogonal to \\(PO\\).  Because \\(P\\) and \\(O\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\) (the centre \\(P\\) of \\((AEFC)\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\), which is perpendicular to \\(MN\\); the centre \\(O\\) is the midpoint of \\(MN\\)), we have  \n\n\\[\nPO\\parallel MN .\n\\]\n\nHence the radical axis of the two circles is the line through \\(A\\) **perpendicular** to \\(MN\\).  Since \\(A\\) belongs to the line \\(AP\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\nAP\\perp MN . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nNow consider the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\).  By (3) \\(\\ell\\) is also perpendicular to \\(MN\\).  Because \\(H\\) lies inside \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the orthocenter is reflected across \\(MN\\) onto a point \\(H'\\) of the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) (see (2)), the line \\(\\ell\\) meets the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) at a point \\(T\\) that is the second intersection of the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) with \\((PMN)\\).  The segment \\(HT\\) is a diameter of the nine‑point circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\); in particular \\(T\\) is the point of tangency of the external common tangent of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) (the unique line through a point of \\((PMN)\\) that is perpendicular to \\(MN\\)).  Therefore \\(\\ell\\) is exactly the external common tangent of the two given circles. ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3.  The homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio  \n\n\\[\nk=\\frac{AE}{AP}=\\frac{AF}{AP}\n\\]\n\nmaps the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \n\nFrom Lemma 1 we have \\(PA=PE=PF\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{AE}{AP}= \\frac{AF}{AP}=k>0 .\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(\\mathcal H\\) as the homothety centred at \\(A\\) with ratio \\(k\\).  By construction  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(P)=E,\\qquad \\mathcal H(O)=\\mathcal H\\bigl(\\text{midpoint of }MN\\bigr)=\\text{midpoint of }EF .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(B\\) is the second intersection of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the lines \\(AB\\) and \\(AE\\) (resp. \\(AF\\)) are symmetric with respect to the internal and external homothety centres of the two circles.  The homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) fixes the line \\(AB\\) (its centre is \\(A\\) and the ratio is the same on both sides of \\(A\\)), hence  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H(B)=B .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(\\mathcal H\\) sends the three non‑collinear points \\(P,M,N\\) to the three non‑collinear points \\(E,B,F\\).  A homothety maps a circle to a circle and preserves concyclicity; therefore the image of the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) under \\(\\mathcal H\\) is precisely the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\). ∎\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof\n\nBy Lemma 2 the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(H\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is the external common tangent of the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\).  Lemma 3 tells us that the homothety \\(\\mathcal H\\) with centre \\(A\\) and ratio \\(k\\) sends the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) onto the circumcircle \\((BEF)\\).  A homothety maps a tangent line to a tangent line; consequently \\(\\mathcal H(\\ell)\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal H\\) is centred at \\(A\\), it does not change the direction of any line passing through \\(A\\); in particular the image of a line parallel to \\(AP\\) is again a line parallel to \\(AP\\).  Hence \\(\\mathcal H(\\ell)\\) is a line parallel to \\(AP\\) that is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nBut \\(\\ell\\) itself already passes through \\(H\\) and is parallel to \\(AP\\); therefore \\(\\ell\\) coincides with its own image under \\(\\mathcal H\\).  Consequently \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).\n\nThus the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\), as was to be shown. ∎"
