to\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }\\triangle BEF\\;}. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Two auxiliary circles\n\nFrom (1) we obtain\n\n\\[\nPA=PE=PF .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nHence the four points \\(A,E,F,P\\) are concyclic; denote this circle by\n\\(\\omega_{P}\\) (its centre is \\(P\\) and its radius is \\(PA\\)).\n\nBecause \\(PA=PC=PD\\) as well, the points \\(A,C,D,P\\) are also concyclic; we\ndenote this circle by \\(\\omega_{C}\\).\n\nThus we have two circles with the same centre \\(P\\):\n\n\\[\n\\omega_{P}= (A,E,F,P),\\qquad \\omega_{C}= (A,C,D,P). \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Angles that involve the points \\(B,E,F\\)\n\nThe line \\(AB\\) is the **radical axis** of the original circles \\(\\Omega\\) and\n\\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the powers of any point on \\(AB\\) with respect to\n\\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are equal.  In particular\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Omega}(A)=0=\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\Gamma}(A). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(E\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(F\\in\\Gamma\\), the equalities (8) give\n\n\\[\nAE\\cdot AC = AF\\cdot AD .\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nUsing (1) we have \\(AC=AD\\); therefore (9) reduces to\n\n\\[\nAE = AF .\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus the chords \\(AE\\) and \\(AF\\) of the two original circles have the same\nlength.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Equality of two angles\n\nConsider the quadrilateral \\(A,E,P,F\\).  It is cyclic (by (6)), and\n\\(PA=PE=PF\\); consequently it is an **isosceles** cyclic quadrilateral, and\nits base angles are equal:\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEP = \\angle FAP .\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(AP\\perp MN\\) (Section 2) and \\(C,D\\) lie on \\(MN\\), the points\n\\(C\\) and \\(D\\) are symmetric with respect to the line \\(AP\\).  Hence\n\n\\[\n\\angle AEC = \\angle FAD .\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nNow \\(B\\) belongs to both original