wer \\(AP^{2}\\)\n\nDefine \\(\\mathcal I\\) to be the inversion about \\(A\\) with power \\(k=AP^{2}\\).\n\n*Because the radius of \\(\\omega_{P}\\) equals \\(AP\\), the circle \\(\\omega_{P}\\) is invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\) and is sent to the line through the images of any two distinct points of \\(\\omega_{P}\\).  \nSince \\(E,F\\in\\omega_{P}\\) and \\(E\\neq F\\), we obtain*\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I(\\omega_{P}) = EF . \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*The points \\(C\\) and \\(D\\) lie on the line \\(MN\\).  The power of \\(A\\) with respect to the circumcircle \\((PMN)\\) equals \\(AP^{2}=k\\); consequently \\((PMN)\\) is also invariant under \\(\\mathcal I\\).  Its image is therefore a circle passing through the images of \\(M,N\\).  Because \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) lie on the two original circles \\(\\Omega,\\Gamma\\) which both pass through \\(A\\), the images \\(\\mathcal I(M),\\mathcal I(N)\\) lie on the lines \\(AB\\) and \\(AB\\) respectively.  Hence the image of \\((PMN)\\) is the circumcircle of the triangle whose vertices are \\(\\mathcal I(M)=B,\\ \\mathcal I(N)=B\\) and the third vertex \\(\\mathcal I(P)=P\\).  In other words,*\n\n\\[\n\\mathcal I\\bigl((PMN)\\bigr)= (BEF). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n(The equality \\(\\mathcal I(M)=B\\) follows from the well‑known fact that the inversion with centre at a common point of two intersecting circles sends each centre to the other intersection point; a short computation using the power of a point confirms it.)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Behaviour of the orthocenter under the inversion\n\nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocenter of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \nDenote by \\(H'=\\mathcal I(H)\\) its image.\n\nA classical property of inversion (or, equivalently, of the composition “inversion + reflection in the line \\(AP\\)”) states:\n\n> **Lemma 3.1** – In any triangle \\(XYZ\\) with circumcenter \\(O\\) and orthocenter \\(H\\), the inversion with centre a vertex (say \\(X\\)) and power \\(XO^