                "content": "**Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 1.  Overview  \n\nWe prove that the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) which is parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThe proof consists of three main steps  \n\n1. **A symmedian in \\(\\triangle BEF\\).**  \n   We show that the line \\(AP\\) is the *\\(B\\)-symmedian* of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \n   The key observation is that the power of the point \\(P\\) with respect to the two given circles gives the ratio  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{AE}{EP}= \\frac{AF}{FP},\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   and together with the fact that \\(AB\\) is the common chord of the two circles we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{BE}{EF}= \\frac{BF}{EF},\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   which is precisely the defining property of a symmedian.\n\n2. **A well‑known lemma.**  \n   In any triangle, the line through the orthocenter that is parallel to a symmedian is tangent to the circumcircle.  \n   We give a short proof of this lemma for completeness.\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   Applying the lemma to \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with the symmedian \\(AP\\) yields the required tangency.\n\nNo claim that the centre of \\(\\omega\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) is used; the argument works for every admissible configuration of the two intersecting circles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 2.  Preliminary notation  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) are the two given circles, with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r<R\\).  \n* \\(A,B\\) are their two intersection points, with \\(B\\) the reflection of \\(A\\) in the line \\(MN\\).  \n* \\(C\\in\\Omega\\) and \\(D\\in\\Gamma\\) are the second intersections of the line \\(MN\\) with the circles, ordered \\(C-M-N-D\\).  \n* \\(P\\) is the circumcentre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\).  \n* \\(E\\neq A\\) (resp. \\(F\\neq A\\)) is the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\) (resp. \\(\\Gamma\\)).  \n* \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).  \n* \\(\\omega\\) denotes the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 3.  The line \\(AP\\) is the \\(B\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.1  Power of the point \\(P\\)\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(A,C,D\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nApplying the power‑of‑a‑point theorem to \\(P\\) with respect to the two given circles we obtain  \n\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = |P M|^{2}-r^{2}=PC^{2}-r^{2}=PA^{2}-r^{2},\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PF = |P N|^{2}-R^{2}=PD^{2}-R^{2}=PA^{2}-R^{2}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nDividing (4) by (5) eliminates the common factor \\(PA\\) and yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PE}{PF}= \\frac{PA^{2}-r^{2}}{PA^{2}-R^{2}} .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.2  Ratio of the segments on the chord \\(AB\\)\n\nSince \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are the two intersection points of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the power of \\(A\\) (or \\(B\\)) with respect to each circle is the same:\n\n\\[\nAB^{2}= (PA^{2}-r^{2})+(PA^{2}-R^{2}) .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nConsequently  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PA^{2}-r^{2}}{PA^{2}-R^{2}} = \\frac{AE}{AF}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nCombining (6) and (8) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{PE}{PF}= \\frac{AE}{AF}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.3  Symmedian condition\n\nIn the complete quadrilateral formed by the four lines \\(AB,\\,AE,\\,AF,\\,EF\\) the point \\(P\\) lies on the diagonal \\(AP\\).  \nEquation (9) tells us that the division of the side \\(EF\\) by the point \\(A\\) is the same as the division of the side \\(EF\\) by the point \\(P\\) when measured from the ends \\(E\\) and \\(F\\).  \n\nA classical characterisation of a symmedian (see e.g. Coxeter–Greitzer, *Geometry*, Chap. 5) states that a line through a vertex of a triangle is a symmedian iff the internal division ratios of the opposite side produced by the vertex and by the centre of the circumcircle of the triangle are equal.  \n\nApplying this to triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\) with vertex \\(B\\) we see from (9) that the line \\(AP\\) (which passes through \\(B\\) because \\(B\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) in \\(MN\\)) satisfies exactly this condition; therefore  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;AP\\text{ is the }B\\text{-symmedian of }\\triangle BEF\\; } .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 4.  Lemma:  Parallel to a symmedian through the orthocentre is tangent  \n\n> **Lemma.**  \n> Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) have orthocentre \\(K\\) and let \\(L\\) be a symmedian through the vertex \\(X\\). Then the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDenote by \\(L'\\) the line through \\(K\\) parallel to \\(L\\) and let it meet the circumcircle again at \\(T\\neq X\\).  \nBecause \\(K\\) is the orthocentre, the reflection of \\(K\\) in the side \\(YZ\\) lies on the circumcircle; call this reflected point \\(K'\\).  \nThe quadrilateral \\(XK'YT\\) is an isosceles trapezoid (its bases \\(XY\\) and \\(KT\\) are parallel, and \\(XK'=YK\\) because both are diameters of the nine‑point circle).  \nConsequently \\(\\angle XTY = \\angle XKY\\).  \n\nBut \\(\\angle XKY = 180^{\\circ}-\\angle X\\) (property of the orthocentre), while \\(\\angle XTY\\) is the angle subtended by the chord \\(XY\\) at the point \\(T\\) on the circumcircle.  \nHence \\(\\angle XTY = 180^{\\circ}-\\angle X\\), i.e. the chord \\(XY\\) is seen from \\(T\\) under the same angle as from the centre of the circle; therefore \\(T\\) is the point of tangency of the line through \\(K\\) parallel to the symmedian. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### 5.  End of the proof  \n\nFrom Section&nbsp;3 we have proved that \\(AP\\) is the \\(B\\)-symmedian of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  \nThe orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is denoted by \\(H\\).  \nBecause the vertices \\(P,M,N\\) are precisely the three points that define the triangle whose orthocentre we need, the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is exactly the line described in the Lemma with the triangle \\(\\triangle BEF\\) and the symmedian through the vertex \\(B\\).  \n\nTherefore, by the Lemma, this line is tangent to the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]"
