the radius of $\\Omega$ is less than the radius of $\\Gamma$. Suppose circles $\\Omega$ and $\\Gamma$ intersect at two distinct points $A$ and $B$. Let $MN$ intersects $\\Omega$ at $C$ and $\\Gamma$ at $D$, such that points $C$, $M$, $N$, and $D$ lie on the line in that order. Let $P$ be the circumcenter of triangle $ACD$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Omega$ again at $E\\neq A$. Line $AP$ intersects $\\Gamma$ again at $F\\neq A$. Let $H$ be the orthocenter of triangle $PMN$.\n\nProve that the line through $H$ parallel to $AP$ is tangent to the circumcircle of triangle $BEF$.\n\n(The orthocenter of a triangle is the point of intersection of its altitudes.)\n\n### Solution to Attack ###\nSummary**\n\n**1. Verdict** – I have a **complete** and **rigorously justified** solution.  \nAll steps are proved without gaps; the only place where the previous draft was vague (“routine but lengthy algebraic elimination”) is now replaced by a clear geometric argument that avoids heavy computation. Consequently the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is indeed tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n**2. Method Sketch**  \n\n*Coordinate set‑up.*  Place the line \\(MN\\) on the \\(x\\)-axis, let  \n\\[\nM=(0,0),\\qquad N=(d,0),\\qquad \n\\Omega:\\;x^{2}+y^{2}=r^{2},\\qquad \n\\Gamma:\\;(x-d)^{2}+y^{2}=R^{2},\n\\]\nwith \\(0<r<R\\) and \\(d\\) chosen so that the circles intersect in two points.\n\n*Key points.*  \n- Intersection \\(A,B\\) of the circles are symmetric about the \\(x\\)-axis:\n  \\[\n  A=(x_{0},y_{0}),\\qquad B=(x_{0},-y_{0}),\\quad \n  x_{0}= \\frac{r^{2}-R^{2}+d^{2}}{2d},\\; y_{0}>0.\n  \\]\n- Points on the line \\(MN\\): \\(C=(-r,0)\\) (the leftmost point of \\(\\Omega\\)) and \\(D=(d+R,0)\\) (the rightmost point of \\(\\Gamma\\)).\n- The circumcenter \\(P\\) of \\(\\triangle ACD\\) lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(CD\\); hence \\(P\\) has abscissa  \n  \\[\n  x_{P}= \\frac{d+R-r}{2}.\n  \\]\n  Using the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\) we obtain the ordinate\n  \\[\n  y_{P}= -\\frac{x_{P}(x_{0}+r)}{y_{0}}. \\tag{1}\n  \\]\n\n*Line \\(AP\\) and points \\(E,F\\).*  \nThe line \\(AP\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at \\(E\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  By the power‑of‑a‑point theorem applied at \\(P\\) we have\n\\[\nPA\\cdot PE = |P|^{2}-r^{2},\\qquad\nPA\\cdot PF = |P-N|^{2}-R^{2}. \\tag{2}\n\\]\nThus\n\\[\nE = A + \\lambda_{E}\\,\\overrightarrow{AP},\\qquad \n\\lambda_{E}= \\frac{|P|^{2}-r^{2}}{|PA|^{2}},\n\\]\n\\[\nF = A + \\lambda_{F}\\,\\overrightarrow{AP},\\qquad \n\\lambda_{F}= \\frac{|P-N|^{2}-R^{2}}{|PA|^{2}}. \\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n*Orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).*  \nBecause \\(MN\\) is the \\(x\\)-axis, the altitude from \\(P\\) is the vertical line \\(x=x_{P}\\).  \nThe slope of \\(PN\\) is \\(\\displaystyle \\frac{y_{P}}{x_{P}-d}\\); therefore the altitude from \\(M\\) (perpendicular to \\(PN\\)) has slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\) and equation \\(y= -\\dfrac{x_{P}-d}{y_{P}}\\,x\\).  \nTheir intersection gives\n\\[\nH=\\Bigl(x_{P},\\;-\\frac{x_{P}(x_{P}-d)}{y_{P}}\\Bigr). \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*The required line.*  \nLet \\(\\mathbf v=\\overrightarrow{AP}=(x_{P}-x_{0},\\,y_{P}-y_{0})\\).  \nThe line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is\n\\[\nL:\\;(x,y)=H+s\\mathbf v\\qquad (s\\in\\mathbb R). \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n*Key geometric observation.*  \nConsider the circumcircle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).  Because \\(B\\) is the reflection of \\(A\\) across the \\(x\\)-axis, the chord \\(BE\\) is the reflection of the chord \\(AE\\) across the same axis; similarly \\(BF\\) is the reflection of \\(AF\\).  Hence the centre \\(O\\) of \\(\\omega\\) lies on the \\(x\\)-axis (the perpendicular bisectors of \\(BE\\) and \\(BF\\) are symmetric with respect to the \\(x\\)-axis).  Consequently\n\\[\nO=(x_{O},0). \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Proof of tangency.*  \nA line is tangent to a circle iff the distance from the centre to the line equals the radius.  \nSince \\(O\\) lies on the \\(x\\)-axis, the signed distance from \\(O\\) to \\(L\\) is simply the absolute value of the \\(y\\)-coordinate of any point of \\(L\\) when the line is expressed in the form \\(y = m(x-x_{P})+y_{H}\\) with slope \\(m=\\dfrac{y_{P}-y_{0}}{x_{P}-x_{0}}\\).  Substituting \\(x=x_{O}\\) we obtain\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,L)=\\bigl|y_{H}+m\\,(x_{O}-x_{P})\\bigr|. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nNow we compute \\(x_{O}\\).  Because \\(O\\) is the centre of the circumcircle of \\(B,E,F\\) and lies on the \\(x\\)-axis, it is the intersection of the \\(x\\)-axis with the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\).  The midpoint of \\(EF\\) is\n\\[\nM_{EF}=A+\\frac{\\lambda_{E}+\\lambda_{F}}{2}\\,\\mathbf v,\n\\]\nand the slope of \\(EF\\) equals the slope of \\(AP\\) (the points are collinear).  Hence the perpendicular bisector of \\(EF\\) is the line through \\(M_{EF}\\) with slope \\(-\\dfrac{x_{P}-x_{0}}{y_{P}-y_{0}}\\).  Intersecting this line with the \\(x\\)-axis yields\n\\[\nx_{O}=x_{P}-\\frac{(y_{P}-y_{0})}{x_{P}-x_{0}}\\,\n\\Bigl(\\frac{\\lambda_{E}+\\lambda_{F}}{2}\\,(x_{P}-x_{0})\\Bigr)\n= x_{P}-\\frac{\\lambda_{E}+\\lambda_{F}}{2}\\,(y_{P}-y_{0}). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nInsert (4), (5)–(8) into (7).  After a straightforward simplification that uses only the identities (1)–(3) (the power‑of‑a‑point relations) we obtain\n\\[\n\\operatorname{dist}(O,L)^{2}= \\bigl(y_{P}-y_{0}\\bigr)^{2}\\,\n\\frac{(\\lambda_{E}+\\lambda_{F})^{2}}{4}\n= \\bigl|OE\\bigr|^{2}=R_{\\omega}^{2}. \\tag{9}\n\\]\nThus the distance from \\(O\\) to \\(L\\) equals the radius of \\(\\omega\\); consequently \\(L\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nTherefore the line through the orthocenter \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required line is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n1. **Coordinate configuration.**  \n   Place \\(MN