rcum‑circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)) is\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{Pow}_{\\omega}(S)=SM\\cdot SN-SP^{2}=0 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(S\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The line \\(AP\\) is the Simson line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with respect to \\(S\\)\n\nFor a point on the circum‑circle of a triangle, the feet of the perpendiculars to the three sides are collinear (Simson line).  We determine these three feet.\n\n* **Foot on \\(MN\\).**  \n  Because \\(MN\\) is the radical axis, the projection of \\(S\\) onto \\(MN\\) is the midpoint of the common chord \\(AB\\).  This midpoint is precisely the point \\(A\\).  Hence the foot on \\(MN\\) is \\(A\\).\n\n* **Foot on \\(PM\\).**  \n  From (3) we have \\(\\angle (SC,AP)=90^{\\circ}\\); therefore the line through \\(S\\) perpendicular to \\(PM\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), namely \\(E\\).  Thus the foot on \\(PM\\) is \\(E\\).\n\n* **Foot on \\(PN\\).**  \n  Similarly, \\(\\angle (SD,AP)=90^{\\circ}\\); the line through \\(S\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\), namely \\(F\\).  Hence the foot on \\(PN\\) is \\(F\\).\n\nThe three feet are \\(A,E,F\\) and they lie on the line \\(AP\\).  Consequently\n\n\\[\nAP\\text{ is the Simson line of }\\triangle PMN\\text{ with respect to }S .\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Simson‑line tangency theorem\n\n**Theorem (Simson‑line tangency).**  \nLet \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) be any triangle, let \\(U\\) be a point on its circum‑circle, and let \\(L\\) be the Simson line of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) with respect to \\(U\\).  If \\(H\\) is the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\), then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(L\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of the triangle formed by the three feet of the Simson line.\n\nApply the theorem to 