ree feet for the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) and the point \\(S\\).\n\n* **Foot on \\(MN\\).**  \n  Because \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\), the powers of \\(S\\) with respect to the two circles are equal (see (3.2)). Hence the perpendicular from \\(S\\) to \\(MN\\) meets \\(MN\\) at the midpoint \\(X\\) of the common chord \\(AB\\). Consequently the foot on \\(MN\\) is \\(X\\).\n\n* **Foot on \\(PM\\).**  \n  From (3.3) we have \\(\\angle (SP,PM)=90^{\\circ}\\); therefore the perpendicular from \\(S\\) to \\(PM\\) meets the line \\(PM\\) at the point where the line \\(AP\\) meets the small circle again, i.e. at \\(E\\). (Indeed, \\(E\\) is defined as the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\); the quadrilateral \\(ASPE\\) is cyclic, so \\(\\angle SPE=90^{\\circ}\\).)\n\n* **Foot on \\(PN\\).**  \n  The same argument, using the cyclic quadrilateral \\(ASPF\\), shows that the perpendicular from \\(S\\) to \\(PN\\) meets \\(PN\\) at \\(F\\).\n\nThus the three feet are \\(X\\) (on \\(MN\\)), \\(E\\) (on \\(PM\\)) and \\(F\\) (on \\(PN\\)).  \nSince \\(\\angle (XE,XF)=\\angle (AP,AP)=0\\), the three points are collinear; the line through them is parallel to \\(AP\\). Consequently\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The Simson line of }\\triangle PMN\\text{ with respect to }S\\text{ is the line }\\,\\ell\\; \\text{through }X,E,F,\\;\n\\ell\\parallel AP.}\\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  A classical Simson‑line tangency theorem  \n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) be any triangle, \\(U\\) a point on its circum‑circle, and let \\(L\\) be the Simson line of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) with respect to \\(U\\).  Denote by \\(D_X,D_Y,D_Z\\) the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(U\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) (so \\(L=D_XD_YD_Z\\)).  Let \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).  Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(L\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle D_XD_YD_Z\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nBecause \\(U\\) lies on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\), the reflections of \\(H\\) in the three sides of the triangle lie on that circum‑circle.  In particular the reflection of \\(H\\) in \\(YZ\\) is the point \\(U'\\) such that \\(U'D_X=U'D_Y\\); similarly for the other sides.  Hence the quadrilateral \\(U D_X D_Y D_Z\\) is a rectangle, so \\(U\\) is the centre of the circle through \\(D_X,D_Y,D_Z\\).  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(L\\) meets the line \\(D_XD_Y\\) at a point \\(T\\) for which \\(UT\\) is a radius of that circle; therefore the line is tangent at \\(T\\). ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Application to the present configuration  \n\nApply the theorem of §5 to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with the point \\(U=S\\).  \nFrom (4.1) the Simson line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with respect to \\(S\\) is the line \\(\\ell\\) through \\(X,E,F\\) and \\(\\ell\\parallel AP\\).  \nThe three feet are precisely the points \\(X,E,F\\); consequently the circum‑circle of the “Simson triangle’’ is the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XEF\\).\n\nTherefore the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(\\ell\\) – i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  From \\(\\triangle XEF\\) to \\(\\triangle BEF\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); consequently the points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\).  \nThe circle \\((XEF)\\) is invariant under this symmetry because the two original circles are interchanged by the reflection in \\(MN\\) while the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on them.  Hence the symmetric point of \\(A\\) with respect to \\(MN\\), namely \\(B\\), also belongs to \\((XEF)\\).  Thus\n\n\\[\n\\box