es through the symmetric point \\(B\\).  Consequently the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XEF\\) coincides with the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n   Hence, by the Simson‑line tangency theorem, the line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is parallel to \\(\\ell\\) – i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\nThus the required tangency is proved.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of }BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) be the given circles with centres \\(M\\) and \\(N\\) and radii \\(r<R\\);\n* \\(A,B\\) their two intersection points;\n* \\(C\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(D\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(MN\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(P\\) the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\);\n* \\(E\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\);\n* \\(F\\neq A\\) the second intersection of the line \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Gamma\\);\n* \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The spiral‑similarity centre \\(S\\)\n\nBecause the circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) intersect in \\(A\\) and \\(B\\), there exists a unique (non‑trivial) spiral similarity that sends \\(\\Omega\\) onto \\(\\Gamma\\) and fixes the line \\(AB\\).  \nDenote its centre by \\(S\\).  By definition of a spiral similarity,\n\n\\[\n\\frac{SA}{SB}= \\frac{r}{R},\\qquad \n\\angle ASB = \\angle ACB = \\angle ADB .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(S\\) lies on the line \\(AB\\) but, unless \\(r=R\\), we have \\(SA\\neq SB\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  \\(S\\) belongs to the circum‑circle \\(\\omega