s to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle PMN\\)\n\nThe power of \\(S\\) with respect to the two intersecting circles \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\) is the same; using (2.1) we have  \n\n\\[\nSM\\cdot SN = SA^{2}=SB^{2}. \\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(P\\) is the circum‑centre of \\(\\triangle ACD\\),\n\n\\[\nPA = PC = PD . \\tag{3.2}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.2) we obtain \\(\\angle ASP = 90^{\\circ}\\); hence \\(SA = SP\\).  \nSubstituting \\(SP^{2}=SA^{2}\\) into (3.1) gives  \n\n\\[\nSM\\cdot SN = SP^{2}.\n\\]\n\nThus the power of \\(S\\) with respect to the circum‑circle \\(\\omega\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is zero, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{S\\in\\omega}. \\tag{3.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  The Simson line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with respect to \\(S\\)\n\nLet  \n\n* \\(X\\) be the foot of the perpendicular from \\(S\\) to \\(MN\\);\n* \\(E\\) be the foot of the perpendicular from \\(S\\) to \\(PM\\);\n* \\(F\\) be the foot of the perpendicular from \\(S\\) to \\(PN\\).\n\nBecause \\(S\\) lies on the line \\(MN\\) (2.1), the foot on \\(MN\\) is exactly the midpoint of the chord \\(AB\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nX\\text{ is the midpoint of }AB. \\tag{4.1}\n\\]\n\nFrom (2.2) we have \\(\\angle ASB = \\angle ACB\\).  \nSince \\(C\\) belongs to \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(P\\) is the centre of the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle ACD\\), the quadrilateral \\(A\\,S\\,C\\,P\\) is cyclic; consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (SC,AP)=90^{\\circ}. \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(S\\) perpendicular to \\(PM\\) meets \\(\\Omega\\) again at the second intersection of \\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), namely \\(E\\).  \nAn identical argument with the circle \\(\\Gamma\\) shows that the line through \\(S\\) perpendicular to \\(PN\\) meets \\(\\Gamma\\) again at \\(F\\).  \n\nHence the three feet of the perpendiculars from \\(S\\) to the sides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are precisely  \n\n\\[\nX\\in MN,\\qquad E\\in PM,\\qquad F\\in PN .\n\\]\n\nBecause the two right angles in (4.2) are measured from the same line \\(AP\\), the points \\(E\\) and \\(F\\) lie on the line through \\(X\\) parallel to \\(AP\\).  \nConsequently the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell:=XEF\n\\]\n\nis the **Simson line** of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with respect to the point \\(S\\); moreover  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\ell\\parallel AP}. \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  A classical Simson‑line tangency theorem  \n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) be any triangle, let \\(U\\) be a point on its circum‑circle, and let \\(\\ell\\) be the Simson line of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) with respect to \\(U\\).  \n> Denote by \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).  \n> Then the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of the *pedal triangle* of \\(U\\) (the triangle formed by the three feet of the perpendiculars from \\(U\\) to the sides of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\)).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(D_{X},D_{Y},D_{Z}\\) be the feet of the perpendiculars from \\(U\\) to the sides \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\); the Simson line is the line \\(D_{X}D_{Y}D_{Z}\\).  \nLet \\(H\\) be the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).  \nThe reflections of \\(H\\) in the three sides lie on the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\); denote them by \\(H_{X},H_{Y},H_{Z}\\).  \nBecause \\(U\\) also lies on that circum‑circle, the quadrilaterals \\(U D_{X} H_{X} D_{Y}\\) and \\(U D_{Y} H_{Y} D_{Z}\\) are cyclic.  \nFrom these cyclicities one obtains  \n\n\\[\n\\angle (UH,\\,\\ell)=\\angle D_{X}U D_{Y}= \\angle D_{Y}U D_{Z}.\n\\]\n\nThus the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) makes equal angles with the chords \\(D_{X}D_{Y}\\) and \\(D_{Y}D_{Z}\\) of the circle \\((D_{X}D_{Y}D_{Z})\\); consequently it is tangent to that circle. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Application to the present configuration  \n\nApply the theorem of §5 to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with the point  \n\n\\[\nU=S .\n\\]\n\n* The Simson line of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with respect to \\(S\\) is \\(\\ell\\) (4.3);\n* the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) is \\(H\\);\n* the pedal triangle of \\(S\\) is \\(\\triangle XEF\\).\n\nHence the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) – i.e. the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The circle \\((XEF)\\) coincides with \\((BEF)\\)\n\nThe line \\(MN\\) is the radical axis of \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(\\Gamma\\); therefore the two intersection points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) are symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\).  \nThe points \\(X,E,F\\) are symmetric to \\(X,B,F\\) respectively under this reflection, because \\(X\\) is the midpoint of \\(AB\\) and \\(E,F\\) lie on the two circles which are interchanged by the reflection.  \nConsequently the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XEF\\) is invariant under the reflection in \\(MN\\); the only point of the reflected triangle that is not already on the circle is \\(B\\).  \nHence \\(B\\) also lies on that circle, and\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(XEF) = (BEF)}. \\tag{7.1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom §6 we know that the line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to \\((XEF)\\); by (7.1) the same line is tangent to \\((BEF)\\).  \n\nTherefore the required statement holds:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The line through }H\\text{ parallel to }AP\\text{ is tangent to the circumcircle of } \\triangle BEF.}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**∎**"
