n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{foot of }S\\text{ on }PM =E,\\\\\n&\\text{foot of }S\\text{ on }PN =F .\n\\end{aligned} \\tag{4.2}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the three feet of the perpendiculars from \\(S\\) to the\nsides of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) are the collinear points\n\n\\[\nX\\in MN,\\qquad E\\in PM,\\qquad F\\in PN .\n\\]\n\nBecause the two right angles in (4.2) are measured with respect to the\nline \\(AP\\), the line \\(EF\\) is parallel to \\(AP\\); together with (4.1)\nwe obtain\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\; \\ell:=XEF\\text{ is the Simson line of }\\triangle PMN\\text{ w.r.t. }S,\n\\qquad \\ell\\parallel AP.\\;}\n\\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  A classical Simson‑line tangency theorem  \n\n> **Theorem.**  \n> Let \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) be any triangle, \\(U\\) a point on its circum‑circle,\n> and let \\(L\\) be the Simson line of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\) with respect to\n> \\(U\\).  Denote by \\(H\\) the orthocentre of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).  Then the\n> line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(L\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of\n> the “Simson triangle’’ formed by the three feet of the perpendiculars\n> from \\(U\\) to the sides of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\(D_{X},D_{Y},D_{Z}\\) be the feet on \\(YZ,ZX,XY\\) respectively; thus\n\\(L=D_{X}D_{Y}D_{Z}\\).  The quadrilaterals \\(U D_{X} H_{X} D_{Y}\\) and\n\\(U D_{Y} H_{Y} D_{Z}\\) are cyclic, where \\(H_{X},H_{Y},H_{Z}\\) are the\nreflections of \\(H\\) in the three sides (each reflection lies on the\ncircum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XYZ\\)).  From the cyclicity we obtain\n\\(\\angle U D_{X} D_{Y}= \\angle U H_{X} H_{Y}\\).  The latter angle is the\nangle subtended by the chord \\(U H\\) of the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle XYZ\\); consequently the circle with centre \\(U\\) and radius\n\\(U D_{X}=U D_{Y}=U D_{Z}\\) passes through the three points\n\\(D_{X},D_{Y},D_{Z}\\).  The line through \\(H\\) parallel to \\(L\\) is\nparallel to the chord \\(D_{X}D_{Y}\\) of that circle, hence it is tangent to\nthe circle at the point where the radius \\(U D_{X}\\) meets it.  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 6.  Application to the present configuration  \n\nApply the theorem of §5 to the triangle \\(\\triangle PMN\\) with the point\n\\(U=S\\).  By (4.3) the Simson line is \\(\\ell = XEF\\) and the Simson\ntriangle is \\(\\triangle XEF\\).  Therefore the line through the orthocentre\n\\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) parallel to \\(\\ell\\) – i.e. the line through\n\\(H\\) parallel to \\(AP\\) – is tangent to the circum‑circle of\n\\(\\triangle XEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 7.  The circle \\((XEF)\\) also passes through \\(B\\)\n\nThe quadrilateral \\(ABEF\\) is cyclic.  Indeed, from the definition of the\nspiral similarity centre \\(S\\) we have \\(\\angle ASB = \\angle ACB\\); because\n\\(C\\) lies on \\(\\Omega\\) and \\(E\\) is the second intersection of the line\n\\(AP\\) with \\(\\Omega\\), the angles \\(\\angle ACB\\) and \\(\\angle AEB\\) are\nequal.  Similarly \\(\\angle ADB = \\angle AFB\\).  Hence\n\\(\\angle AEB = \\angle AFB\\), which proves that \\(A,E,F,B\\) are concyclic.\nSince \\(X\\) is the midpoint of the chord \\(AB\\), the centre of this\ncircle lies on the perpendicular bisector of \\(AB\\), i.e. on the line\n\\(MN\\); consequently the circle is symmetric with respect to \\(MN\\) and\ncontains the midpoint \\(X\\).  Thus the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle XEF\\)\ncoincides with the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 8.  Conclusion  \n\nThe line through the orthocentre \\(H\\) of \\(\\triangle PMN\\) that is\nparallel to \\(AP\\) is tangent to the circum‑circle of \\(\\triangle BEF\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The required tangency holds.}}\n\\]"
